@attribute [Route(FogUrlBuilder.PageRoutes.HELP_ALLIANCE_PROFILE_PATH)]
@using Ingweland.Fog.Application.Core.Helpers
@inherits Ingweland.Fog.WebApp.Client.Components.Pages.Abstractions.FogPageBase

<div class="help-container">
    <h3>Alliance Profile Help Guide</h3>

    <div class="help-info">
        The Alliance Profile page offers a detailed overview of any alliance recorded in the game. It includes visual,
        historical, and statistical data, allowing you to explore the alliance's structure, performance, and changes
        over time.
    </div>

    <div class="help-step">
        <h5>General Information</h5>
        <p>
            The top section of the profile shows:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>Alliance avatar and name</li>
            <li>Current leader (if known)</li>
            <li>World</li>
            <li>Current ranking position and ranking points</li>
            <li>Date of registration</li>
            <li>Date of last data update</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="help-step">
        <h5>Ranking Points Chart</h5>
        <p>
            A monthly chart illustrates the alliance’s progression in ranking points over time. This visual trend helps
            you understand whether the alliance is rising, plateauing, or declining in performance.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="help-step">
        <h5>Name History Timeline</h5>
        <p>
            Alliances may change names throughout their lifetime. The timeline records every known name change along
            with the corresponding dates, giving you a full naming history.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="help-step">
        <h5>Member List</h5>
        <p>
            The bottom section lists all current alliance members. Each member name is clickable and links to the
            <a href="@FogUrlBuilder.PageRoutes.HELP_PLAYER_PROFILE_PATH">Player Profile page</a>, where
            detailed player information can be explored.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="help-step">
        <h5>Alliance Production</h5>
        <p>
            A collapsible table that compares the cities of all current members, grouped by age. Each row shows what
            the city really produces in a day, how it is built, and where it loses output. Use the
            <strong>All</strong>, <strong>Production</strong> and <strong>City build</strong> tabs or the
            column checkboxes to choose what you see; your choice is remembered in this browser. Click a column header
            to sort, and hover over a number to see how it was calculated.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>Daily value</strong> adds food, goods and coins into one number, counted in food:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Food</strong> — what you can realistically collect, not what the game shows. Rural farms
                are counted at about 18 hours of collecting a day (they are ready every 3 hours, and you sleep);
                domestic, luxurious and other food buildings at 22.5 hours.</li>
            <li><strong>Goods</strong> — valued at the food a rural farm of the same age would make on the tiles a
                workshop takes, divided by the goods that workshop makes. Both buildings are taken at the highest
                level of the age and with full culture bonus, straight from the game data.</li>
            <li><strong>Coins</strong> — through sparks: 84,000 coins = 170 sparks and 70 goods = 95 sparks,
                so 1 good ≈ 671 coins. Coins then follow the goods rate into food.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Compare players only within the same age: the goods rate depends on the age. The
            <strong>±</strong> column shows how far a city is above or below the median of its age among the
            alliance members (shown when the age has at least 3 members).
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>Furnace limit</strong> shows how much of the daily limit for turning goods into sparks (7,000
            goods) the city's workshops could fill.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>Without diamonds</strong> removes the advantage bought with diamonds. The first 7 premium
            expansions are not counted, since the diamonds for them can be earned in the game. Each expansion
            above that removes its tiles. Luxurious buildings count only for what they add over a regular
            building: a farm about 12 tiles, a home about 4, a culture site 3. The daily value is then reduced
            by that share of the city.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>Empty</strong> tiles produce nothing; 12 empty tiles are one missing rural farm.
            <strong>Workers</strong> below zero means some farms or workshops stand idle for lack of workers.
            <strong>Workshops</strong> are shown in three groups: current age, up to three ages back, and older.
            <strong>City</strong> shows how the city's tiles are used: administration, barracks, current workshops,
            farms, older workshops, homes and culture; the grey rest is empty.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>Limitations:</strong> the table uses the latest city snapshot stored for each member, so a city
            is only as fresh as that snapshot — check the <strong>City snapshot</strong> column before comparing.
            Members without a snapshot are listed above the table. The value is a comparison score, not an in-game
            exchange. Collection hours, the free expansion threshold and the value of luxurious buildings are
            estimates. Method by Marek Zenft.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>
